{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  // THE GENERIC CONFIG: what `npx wrangler deploy` (or `npm run deploy`) from a checkout and the Deploy
  // to Cloudflare button deploy into YOUR account. The public instance's own config, with its domains
  // and the history behind each setting, is wrangler.production.jsonc, and Workers Builds deploys that
  // file with `-c`. Nothing here may name that instance's zones or resources:
  // test/wrangler-configs.test.mjs reads them out of the production file and fails if one shows up.
  //
  // THE BUTTON IS UNTESTED: nobody has pressed it on a real account. It needs Workers Paid, because
  // Containers do. On Workers Free, change the deploy command on the button's setup page to
  // `npx wrangler deploy -c wrangler.free.jsonc` (also untested; see that file). docs/SELF-HOSTING.md
  // has the whole walk-through.
  //
  // LINE COMMENTS ONLY. The tests strip `//` lines with a regex before JSON.parse, so a /* */ block or a
  // comment after a value breaks the parse.
  //
  // NOT "mbedfx", ON PURPOSE. `wrangler deploy` creates the named Worker if it does not exist and
  // updates it if it does, so a checkout deploying under the production name into the owner's account
  // would replace the public instance's code. Under this name the same mistake makes a second,
  // separate Worker. Rename it freely; the container application's name is derived from it.
  "name": "mbedfx-selfhost",
  // The deploy entry pairs the fetch handler with the Durable Object exports (see src/index.ts).
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-01",
  // OFF, AND SPELLED OUT. Workers Logs would store every request url for days, and on this Worker the
  // url IS the post somebody pasted (.github/SECURITY.md: no user data at rest). An absent block is not off:
  // the field defaults to true for new Workers. Live debugging is `npx wrangler tail`.
  "observability": { "enabled": false },
  // NO ROUTES, so the Worker answers on <name>.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev. To serve your own domain,
  // add it as a custom domain in the dashboard: wrangler publishes routes and custom domains only when
  // this file lists some, so a dashboard domain survives every deploy of this file. Listing one here
  // instead fails test/wrangler-configs.test.mjs, which keeps the generic file deployable anywhere.
  "workers_dev": true,
  // NO `vars`, and dashboard vars survive deploys. Without keep_vars, wrangler treats this file as the
  // whole truth and deletes every plain var set in the dashboard on the next deploy. Every setting the
  // Worker reads has a default (docs/SELF-HOSTING.md, "Settings"); secrets survive either way. Worth
  // setting there once you have a domain: PUBLIC_ORIGIN, your origins with the primary first, and the
  // `d.` name too if you added it as a custom domain. The half-hourly check renders on the primary
  // (unset, on http://localhost), the fediverse guard refuses every entry, and the converter page
  // offers each one. Also OWN_HOSTS for any other domain you serve, SITE_NAME for the name on your
  // cards, and REDDIT_USER_AGENT if you set Reddit credentials. GUEST_HOSTS only if another Worker of
  // yours hands this one its embed paths (src/guesthost.ts).
  "keep_vars": true,
  // The scheduled check: renders a fixed list of known posts through this Worker and keeps a week of
  // results at /_health. The repo's health workflow treats a run older than 120 minutes as stale, so
  // keep the interval if anything reads /_health. In your copy of the repository, set the Actions
  // variable HEALTH_BASE to your primary origin (and HEALTH_GUESTS if you have guest hosts) to turn
  // that workflow on; without it the job skips.
  "triggers": { "crons": ["*/30 * * * *"] },
  "r2_buckets": [
    // OWN NAME, so a deploy into an account that already runs the public instance cannot share its
    // bucket. Wrangler creates the bucket on the first deploy. Nothing in the code deletes from it, and
    // this file cannot express a lifecycle rule, so add the expiry by hand once (it outlasts every TTL
    // in the code, the longest being 30 days):
    //     npx wrangler r2 bucket lifecycle add mbedfx-selfhost-media expire-60d --expire-days 60
    { "binding": "MEDIA_CACHE", "bucket_name": "mbedfx-selfhost-media" }
  ],
  "containers": [
    // NO ENVIRONMENT FROM THE WORKER. src/container.ts sets no envVars, so a RESOLVER_SECRET Worker
    // secret is sent as a header the container ignores. Only the binding reaches the container. See
    // container/README.md, "Deploying it".
    //
    // NO `name`: wrangler derives <worker name>-mediaresolver, so the application follows the Worker
    // name and cannot collide with the one the production file pins.
    //
    // standard-2 (1 vCPU, 6 GiB, 12 GB), as production. The measured yt-dlp extract is 14.1s on basic,
    // 9.1s on standard-1 and 5.7s here, and nothing faster than 1 vCPU helps (the production file has
    // the table). Smaller is cheaper and slower: a first paste is more likely to miss Discord's window
    // and show the cover still.
    //
    // COST. Instances bill memory and disk on provisioned size for as long as they are awake, idle
    // included; src/container.ts sleeps one after 5 minutes without a request. Four slots awake around
    // the clock is modelled at about $166/month on standard-2 and $29 on basic, on top of Workers Paid.
    // A quiet instance costs a fraction of that, but the half-hourly check can wake a container too.
    //
    // max_instances is a ceiling, not a bill. src/worker.ts spreads every container call over
    // RESOLVER_SLOTS (4) names per RESOLVER_GENERATION, so steady state is 4, and after a deploy the
    // previous generation's 4 can stay awake until they sleep. A cap those two sets can reach makes
    // new calls fail with "Maximum number of running container instances exceeded", and videos
    // degrade to stills. 10 is production's headroom.
    { "class_name": "MediaResolver", "image": "./container/Dockerfile", "max_instances": 10, "instance_type": "standard-2" }
  ],
  // The same objects and migrations as production. A fresh account replays every tag in order, and all
  // three are SQLite-backed, which is the only kind a new namespace can be. Migrations are append-only:
  // add a tag, never edit one, and add it to wrangler.production.jsonc and wrangler.free.jsonc too
  // (the tests hold the three files to the same list).
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "MEDIA_RESOLVER", "class_name": "MediaResolver" },
      { "name": "MUX_RUNNER", "class_name": "MuxRunner" },
      { "name": "SMOKE_RUNNER", "class_name": "SmokeRunner" }
    ]
  },
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["MediaResolver"] },
    { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["MuxRunner"] },
    { "tag": "v3", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SmokeRunner"] }
  ],
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./public",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "run_worker_first": true,
    "not_found_handling": "none"
  },
  // Workers AI translates foreign-script posts. Optional in code (Env.AI): remove it and cards still
  // render, untranslated.
  "ai": { "binding": "AI" },
  "analytics_engine_datasets": [
    // OWN NAME, for the same reason as the bucket. docs/METRICS.md queries production's dataset; use
    // this name in their FROM clauses.
    { "binding": "AE", "dataset": "mbedfx_selfhost_counters" }
  ],
  // WORKER PREVIEWS. Workers Builds runs `npx wrangler preview` for branches other than main on this
  // project's own Worker (wrangler.production.jsonc). Whether a Worker made by the Deploy to Cloudflare
  // button gets the same command is unchecked. Where it does, a bare `npx wrangler preview` reads this
  // file, and without a `previews` block wrangler stops with an error. Apart from the assets, a Preview gets
  // none of the bindings above, so one this block leaves out does not exist there. Cloudflare gives
  // each Preview its own Durable Object namespaces and its own container application, named by
  // wrangler, so nothing here has to be created first.
  //
  // NO R2 BUCKET, SO A PREVIEW NEEDS NO SETUP. A Preview's bucket must exist before the Preview runs,
  // and wrangler creates only the top-level one. Pointing this block at the top-level bucket would let
  // a branch's half-finished mux or stored record into the cache your Worker serves. MEDIA_CACHE is
  // optional (Env in src/analytics.ts) and the code checks for it before every use, so a Preview runs
  // without it. What a Preview does differently:
  //   - no muxed video. withResolver in src/worker.ts needs MEDIA_CACHE as well as MEDIA_RESOLVER, so
  //     a video that needs muxing shows its cover still. The container still answers extracts and
  //     /_clients.
  //   - /_health answers 503.
  //   - post records, translations and Reddit archive answers are not stored, so every view fetches
  //     them again.
  // After every Preview wrangler lists MEDIA_CACHE as "configured for your production Worker but not
  // for Previews". Expect it: leaving the bucket out is what this block does. To mux in Previews,
  // create a bucket just for them and add it to the block:
  //     npx wrangler r2 bucket create mbedfx-selfhost-preview-media
  //     "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "MEDIA_CACHE", "bucket_name": "mbedfx-selfhost-preview-media" }],
  //
  // A DATASET OF ITS OWN, created by its first write, keeps branch traffic out of the counters above.
  // No vars, since YT_PROMISE is off when unset, and no secrets, so a Preview runs without credential
  // pools. Cron and routes never apply to a Preview. Workers Logs stay off: when this block sets no
  // observability, wrangler gives the Preview the top-level setting.
  "previews": {
    "analytics_engine_datasets": [
      { "binding": "AE", "dataset": "mbedfx_selfhost_preview_counters" }
    ],
    "ai": { "binding": "AI" },
    "durable_objects": {
      "bindings": [
        { "name": "MEDIA_RESOLVER", "class_name": "MediaResolver" },
        { "name": "MUX_RUNNER", "class_name": "MuxRunner" },
        { "name": "SMOKE_RUNNER", "class_name": "SmokeRunner" }
      ]
    },
    // The same size as the top level, so extracts in a Preview take as long as on the Worker. No
    // `name`: wrangler refuses one here and names each Preview's application itself.
    "containers": [
      { "class_name": "MediaResolver", "image": "./container/Dockerfile", "max_instances": 10, "instance_type": "standard-2" }
    ]
  }
}
